\title{Mode Collapse under Dr.GRPO:\\
\mb{} and Online Verified Maximum Entropy}

\author{Anonymous Authors}

\begin{document}

\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Binary verifier rewards tell a reasoning policy which responses are correct,
but not which correct responses are redundant. Under Dr.GRPO, this missing
signal can produce \emph{mode collapse}: probability concentrates on a strict
subset of correct outcomes even while accuracy is stable or improving. Token
entropy does not diagnose the failure because many strings can encode the same
solution. We formalize collapse on the conditional distribution of
verifier-positive execution modes and introduce \mb, four environments in
which the same executable validator establishes both correctness and mode
identity. We then propose \xdr, an online verified MaxEnt-Dr.GRPO extension.
It builds support only from policy-generated, validator-positive outcomes;
rewards rare and first-seen modes with an open-set advantage; and globally
replays discovered verified exemplars under adaptive mass and balance terms.
At the largest preregistered checkpoint shared by all three seeds of both arms,
\xdr{} improves \texttt{pass@8} over matched Dr.GRPO in all four domains:
$.372\!\to\!.968$, $.608\!\to\!.665$, $.172\!\to\!.740$, and
$.364\!\to\!.779$. More importantly, additional correct modes beyond the first
rise from $.021,.042,.000,.000$ to $1.376,1.010,.216,.026$. This broad
comparison is exploratory because the registered ModeBench campaign was not
completed. A separate terminal MathIR ablation shows that isolated support
discovery raises \texttt{distinct@8} by $.137$ and \texttt{pass@8} by $.027$.
Together, these results support an executable benchmark, an online
outcome-entropy intervention, and causal evidence for separated discovery.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:introduction}

Reinforcement learning with verifiable rewards (RLVR) can make a reasoning
model more accurate while making repeated samples less useful. A binary
verifier rewards every correct response equally. Dr.GRPO removes a length bias
from GRPO, but it does not add a force that distinguishes two correct
solutions \citep{liu2025understanding}. If one correct solution is sampled more
often, it receives more updates; if another disappears from the on-policy
group, it receives none. Eventually, eight draws can become eight renderings of
one outcome. We call this failure \emph{mode collapse under Dr.GRPO}.

The term needs a precise object. Surface strings are not modes: changes in
spacing, derivation length, or prose can leave the executed solution unchanged.
Final answers are not sufficient either: two algorithms can return the same
answer, while two different-looking expressions can be algebraic aliases. For
prompt $x$, we therefore define a mode by an executable validator
$V(y,s_x)$. It returns $\bot$ for an invalid response and a canonical key $c$
for a correct executed outcome. Mode collapse is concentration of the
conditional distribution over these validator-positive keys, not a decrease in
token entropy and not necessarily a decrease in correctness.

This distinction matters for test-time scaling. Parallel sampling,
self-consistency, search, and self-improvement benefit only when extra samples
cover alternatives rather than repeat the same solution
\citep{wang2023selfconsistency,song2025outcome,li2025divergence}. It also changes
how collapse should be measured. Let $D_K(x)$ be the number of distinct correct
keys in $K$ samples and $P_K(x)=\1[D_K(x)>0]$. Then
\[
  \texttt{excess@}K
  :=\E[D_K-P_K]
  =\texttt{distinct@}K-\texttt{pass@}K.
\]
A policy that produces at most one correct mode has \texttt{excess@}$K=0$
regardless of its accuracy. This simple diagnostic is the organizing metric of
the paper. Figure~\ref{fig:story} grounds it in matched model trajectories:
Dr.GRPO's correct samples rise to 32 out of 32 precisely while its sampled
correct support contracts to one repeated coloring; under the same checkpoints,
\xdr{} retains multimodal support.

\begin{figure}[H]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{figures/modecollapse_story.pdf}
  \caption{\textbf{Mode-collapse example.}
  (A) The prompt has 12 valid solutions; three are shown as Options A--C.
  Numbers inside circles identify vertices, while circle fill denotes assigned
  color. (B) Separate Dr.GRPO and \xdr{} facets show matched
  Qwen2.5-0.5B-Instruct trajectories (seed 43), never paired bars on one axis.
  Each bar pools four fixed-seed, eight-sample draws: colored segments are
  correct modes, gray is invalid output, and the number above each bar is the
  observed correct-mode count. The x-axis reports optimizer steps and stops at
  step 768, the end of epoch 4; no epoch-5 checkpoint is shown. Dr.GRPO reaches
  32/32 correct but collapses to one mode by step 384 and remains there, while
  \xdr{} retains six modes at step 768. Aggregate results are in
  Table~\ref{tab:main-results} and Figure~\ref{fig:training-dynamics}.
  \label{fig:story}}
\end{figure}

We make the problem experimentally observable with \mb. Its four domains span
a constraint assignment, an arithmetic expression, a program behavior, and an
algebraic procedure. In each, correctness and identity come from the same
executed object. Formatting aliases merge, different valid executions remain
distinct, and training never receives a gold catalogue of modes.

We address the failure with \xdr, short for \emph{executable-mode Dr.GRPO}: an
online verified outcome-MaxEnt extension of Dr.GRPO.\footnote{The ``x'' denotes
executable outcomes. It does not denote the detached candidate-reweighting
surrogate called xDr in preliminary repository experiments; those runs are not
pooled into any result in this paper.} The learner records only correct modes
that its current policy actually generates. An open-set predictive advantage
rewards rare modes, a one-time credit rewards first discovery, and a global
scheduler makes one verified bank act on every optimizer update through
verified-mass and known-mode-balance replay. The task reward and Dr.GRPO
normalization remain unchanged.

Our claims follow the evidence rather than the size of the experiment archive.
The four-domain method comparison is a frozen, three-seed shared-checkpoint
analysis and is explicitly exploratory. A six-run terminal MathIR ablation
supports a narrower causal claim about separated support discovery.

Our contributions are:
\begin{itemize}
  \item an operational definition of mode collapse under Dr.GRPO, including a
  diagnostic that separates correctness from breadth;
  \item \mb, a four-domain executable benchmark in which reward and semantic
  identity are bound to one validation path;
  \item \xdr, an online verified MaxEnt-Dr.GRPO method over policy-discovered
  correct outcomes, without a gold support catalogue; and
  \item an auditable evidence hierarchy: a broad exploratory comparison, a
  positive terminal causal ablation, and explicit exclusion of incomplete or
  failed cohorts.
\end{itemize}

\section{Mode Collapse under Dr.GRPO}
\label{sec:collapse}

\subsection{Correct mass and correct-mode distribution}

For prompt $x$, response $y$, and reference specification $s_x$, let
\[
  V(y,s_x)\in\{\bot\}\cup\mathcal C_x,
  \qquad r(x,y)=\1[V(y,s_x)\neq\bot].
\]
The policy's total correct mass is
$P_\theta^+(x)=\Pr_{Y\sim\pi_\theta}[V(Y,s_x)\neq\bot]$.
Conditional on correctness, its mode distribution is
\[
  p_\theta^+(c\mid x)
  =\Pr[V(Y,s_x)=c\mid V(Y,s_x)\neq\bot].
\]
We say training exhibits \emph{mode collapse} on $x$ when probability within
$p_\theta^+$ concentrates on a strict subset of $\mathcal C_x$ while
$P_\theta^+$ remains comparable or increases. Low accuracy alone is not mode
collapse; a high-accuracy policy supported on one correct mode is.

Two population quantities describe the failure: conditional verified-mode
entropy
$H_\theta^+(x)=-\sum_c p_\theta^+(c\mid x)\log p_\theta^+(c\mid x)$
and concentration $\max_c p_\theta^+(c\mid x)$. They are unavailable when the
support is unknown, so evaluation uses samples:
\[
\begin{aligned}
  \texttt{pass@}K &= \E_x\E[P_K(x)],\\
  \texttt{distinct@}K &= \E_x\E[D_K(x)],\\
  \texttt{excess@}K &= \E_x\E[D_K(x)-P_K(x)].
\end{aligned}
\]
The first measures whether search finds any correct mode; the second counts
correct modes; the third removes the first success and isolates breadth beyond
it. We also report \texttt{mean@}$K$, the fraction of the $K$ draws that are
correct, and greedy \texttt{pass@1}. No one metric substitutes for the others.

\subsection{Why equal reward does not preserve equal modes}

For a group of $G$ responses, Dr.GRPO uses the task advantage
\[
  A_i^{\mathrm{task}}=r_i-\frac{1}{G}\sum_{j=1}^G r_j
\]
with one shared maximum-length normalization. Correct responses in the same
group receive the same task advantage. That symmetry removes a preference
based on identity, but it does not preserve mode ratios.

A categorical toy model makes the feedback explicit. Let $z_c$ be a logit for
correct mode $c$, $p_c=\operatorname{softmax}(z)_c$, and
$P^+=\sum_{c\in\mathcal C}p_c$. Gradient ascent on expected binary reward gives
\[
  \dot z_c=\frac{\partial P^+}{\partial z_c}=p_c(1-P^+),
  \qquad
  \frac{d}{dt}\log\frac{p_c}{p_d}
  =(p_c-p_d)(1-P^+).
\]
While incorrect mass remains, the more common of two equally rewarded correct
modes receives the larger logit update. Finite on-policy groups sharpen the
asymmetry: an absent rare mode receives no update, and an all-correct group has
zero centered task advantage. Thus the first successful mode can become a
self-reinforcing and eventually locally frozen solution. This idealized
calculation is a mechanism, not a claim that a language model is a categorical
bandit; it explains why binary reward alone supplies no restoring force. Recent
analyses reach related conclusions through outcome-frequency amplification,
divergence choice, or set-level policy geometry
\citep{sinha2026expected,li2026distribution,li2026setpo}.

Appendix~\ref{app:theory} makes the mechanism precise. Under the infinitesimal
on-policy mean dynamics, Theorem~\ref{thm:grpo-collapse} shows that generic
binary-reward GRPO and Dr.GRPO trajectories select one correct mode. After the
verified bank covers the correct support, Theorem~\ref{thm:xdr-balance} and
Corollary~\ref{cor:xdr-no-collapse} show that xDr.GRPO reverses the unstable
mode-ratio dynamics and rules out collapse in the categorical abstraction.
The coverage and optimization assumptions are stated explicitly; the theorem
does not pretend that an arbitrary neural policy must discover an unseen mode.

\section{\mb: Executable Mode Measurement}
\label{sec:modebench}

\subsection{Benchmark contract}

A benchmark instance is mode-verifiable only when $V$ satisfies two
requirements. First, it validates the object the model actually generated,
not a model-supplied claim about that object. Second, the canonical key is
derived from the same execution that established correctness. Cosmetic aliases
must merge; different executed outcomes must remain distinct. This contract
prevents wrong-but-novel responses from receiving diversity credit and prevents
formatting noise from masquerading as solution breadth.

\begin{table}[H]
  \centering
  \small
  \setlength{\tabcolsep}{4pt}
  \begin{tabularx}{\linewidth}{@{}lYYYl@{}}
    \toprule
    \textbf{Domain} & \textbf{Generated object / check} &
    \textbf{Mode key} & \textbf{Why it is semantic} & \textbf{train/eval} \\
    \midrule
    Graph coloring & Complete assignment; check fixed colors and every edge &
    Color vector & Spacing aliases merge; valid assignments differ & 192/96 \\
    Countdown & Parse AST; enforce exact operands; execute rationally &
    Normalized AST & Commutative aliases merge; expression trees differ & 384/128 \\
    Python factors & Call restricted function in an external worker &
    Returned integer vector & Source aliases merge; behaviors differ & 384/128 \\
    MathIR & Execute a bounded algebra action program exactly &
    Equation-state trajectory & Endpoints can match while routes differ & 384/128 \\
    \bottomrule
  \end{tabularx}
  \caption{\textbf{The \mb{} contract.} Correctness and mode identity are
  outputs of the same executable validation path. No exhaustive mode catalogue
  initializes training.}
  \label{tab:tasks}
\end{table}

\paragraph{Graph coloring.}
Each prompt specifies a graph, a partial three-color assignment, and hidden
vertices. The validator reconstructs the complete assignment, preserves shown
colors, and rejects every monochromatic edge. The full color vector is the key.

\paragraph{Countdown.}
Each prompt gives three distinct integers and a target. A response must use
each integer exactly once with $+,-,\times,\div$ and parentheses. The parser
executes with exact rational arithmetic. Canonicalization sorts commutative
children, merging syntactic aliases but retaining genuinely different trees.

\paragraph{Python factors.}
Each prompt asks for \texttt{lambda n: EXPR} on four composite inputs. A
syntax-restricted external Python worker requires every returned $d$ to satisfy
$1<d<n$ and $n\bmod d=0$. The complete output vector is the key, so different
program texts with identical behavior merge. A prompt with input tuple
$(n_1,\ldots,n_m)$ has
$\prod_j|D(n_j)|$ exact modes, where $D(n)$ is the set of proper nontrivial
divisors. The released rows contain 16--3,600 modes each.

\paragraph{MathIR.}
Each prompt shows a linear equation and at most eight lettered transformations.
The model emits at most four actions. An interpreter applies each action to both
sides and normalizes with exact rational arithmetic after every step. The key
is the resulting state trajectory. Each prompt has five exhaustively enumerated
valid routes, withheld from the training policy and bank.

\section{\xdr: Online Verified MaxEnt-Dr.GRPO}
\label{sec:method}

\xdr{} is an outcome-level maximum-entropy surrogate layered on Dr.GRPO. It
does not maximize token entropy and does not assume the valid support is known.
It couples three signals: online rare/new-mode advantages, verified replay, and
self-referenced adaptive coefficients. The task advantage remains unchanged.

\subsection{Online verified support}

For each prompt $x$, the learner maintains a bank $\Bpos$ of keys that the
current policy generated and $V$ accepted. It starts empty. Every group is
scored against one immutable pre-group snapshot, and new keys are committed
after the whole group, making novelty invariant to row order. Admission is
fail-closed at the intersection of positive rollout reward and executable
canonical validation. A disagreement receives no key and is recorded.

The matched Dr.GRPO control runs the same validator and passive counts with all
auxiliary coefficients set to zero. Thus discovery telemetry is comparable
without changing its optimization objective.

\subsection{Open-set rare-mode and discovery advantages}

For a verified mode $a$, let $c_x(a)$ combine its historical count with its
leave-one-out multiplicity among the other verified responses in the group.
With pseudocount $\lambda=1$, the detached predictive distribution includes one
structural unseen bucket $\varnothing$:
\[
  q_x(a)=\frac{c_x(a)+\lambda}
  {\sum_b c_x(b)+\lambda(K_x+1)},\qquad
  q_x(\varnothing)=\frac{\lambda}
  {\sum_b c_x(b)+\lambda(K_x+1)}.
\]
Here $K_x$ is the number of explicitly counted keys. The unseen bucket says the
observed bank is not assumed complete.

With surprisal clip $S=5$, define
\[
  h_i=\min\{-\log q_x(a_i),S\},\qquad
  \bar h_q=\E_{a\sim q_x}\min\{-\log q_x(a),S\},
\]
\[
  A_i^{\mathrm{rare}}=\beta_t\frac{h_i-\bar h_q}{S}.
\]
Centering under $q_x$, rather than under the empirical group, keeps a carrier
when the current correct samples all share one mode but the bank records more.

Let $\mathcal N_x$ be the verified keys absent from the pre-group bank and
$m_x(a)$ their current-group multiplicity. A first discovery receives
\[
  A_i^{\mathrm{new}}
  =\nu\frac{\1[a_i\in\mathcal N_x]}{m_x(a_i)},\qquad \nu=0.50.
\]
All duplicates of one new key share total credit $\nu$. The actor advantage is
\[
  A_i=A_i^{\mathrm{task}}
  +\1[V(y_i,s_x)\neq\bot]
   \left(A_i^{\mathrm{rare}}+A_i^{\mathrm{new}}\right).
\]
There is no second empirical centering after the mode terms. Invalid responses
receive neither novelty nor entropy pressure.

\subsection{Global verified replay}
\label{sec:replay}

With hundreds of training prompts, a prompt-local discovery may otherwise wait
nearly a pass before acting again. After the first verified bank exists, every
optimizer update therefore schedules exactly one bank in deterministic global
round-robin order. It retains at most one policy-generated exemplar per
observed key, up to 16 keys. Two losses apply:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \emph{verified mass}, uniform exemplar likelihood for every scheduled
  bank, including a singleton, with coefficient $\mu_t$; and
  \item \emph{known-mode balance},
  $\mathrm{KL}(U_{\mathrm{bank}}\|q_{\mathrm{model}})$ for banks with at least
  two observed modes, with coefficient $\alpha_t$.
\end{itemize}
Selection reads only accumulated verified banks and a one-bank compute budget.
It never reads exhaustive support, evaluation outcomes, a desired mode count,
or reference solutions. Before the first discovery, replay is inactive.

\subsection{Projection-free self-reference}

The rare-mode, verified-mass, and balance coefficients each warm up for 64
eligible observations and reference only their own warmup mean. Let $\rho$ be
normalized open-set entropy, $s$ verified-exemplar surprisal, and $h$ scheduled
bank entropy. With EMA decay $.9$,
\[
  \beta_t=.10\frac{\rho_{\mathrm{ref}}}{\rho_{\mathrm{ema}}},\qquad
  \mu_t=.10\frac{s_{\mathrm{ema}}}{s_{\mathrm{ref}}},\qquad
  \alpha_t=.10\frac{h_{\mathrm{ref}}}{h_{\mathrm{ema}}}.
\]
Falling mode entropy increases exploration pressure; rising surprisal on known
correct exemplars increases mass pressure; falling bank entropy increases
balance pressure. No coefficient is projected. This is a stress-test design:
continued collapse remains visible as continued coefficient growth rather than
as silent saturation. Singleton banks are omitted from the balance sensor.

\subsection{Separated-support escape actuator}
\label{sec:actuator}

All balance mechanisms need an alternative to preserve. A registered extension
therefore tests one support-only escape for a singleton bank. It is eligible
only after controller warmup, when the run's own entropy EMA is below its own
warmup reference. A domain-specific, validator-preserving transformation may
propose at most one new verified key. Proposal rows never enter PPO, and
proposal-only support is isolated from the historical count table used by the
on-policy rare/new advantages. It may enter replay support; it joins on-policy
counts only after the neutral policy independently produces and validates it.
A same-plumbing actuator-off arm is the causal comparator.

\section{Related Work}
\label{sec:related}

\paragraph{Verifier-based reasoning RL.}
GRPO and Dr.GRPO train reasoning policies from group-relative outcome rewards
\citep{shao2024deepseekmath,guo2025deepseekr1,liu2025understanding}. Dr.GRPO
addresses response-length normalization; our question is orthogonal: what
happens to probability \emph{within} the set of equally rewarded correct
outcomes?

\paragraph{Diversity collapse and diversity-aware optimization.}
Recent work documents loss of answer or trajectory diversity under reasoning
fine-tuning and RLVR, and intervenes with historical bonuses, within-batch
penalties, pass@$K$ objectives, semantic similarity, divergence changes,
set-level objectives, or distribution matching
\citep{song2025outcome,divpo2025,chen2025passktraining,li2025divergence,
li2026setpo,li2026distribution,sinha2026expected}. These approaches optimize
complementary objects. \mb's distinction is an admission contract: a novelty
key exists only if the same generated object executes correctly. \xdr's bank
is also open-set and policy-generated rather than initialized from a solution
catalogue.

\paragraph{Maximum-entropy control.}
Maximum-entropy RL augments reward with policy entropy and supports adaptive
entropy coefficients \citep{ziebart2008maximum,levine2018control,haarnoja18b,
haarnoja2018algorithms}. \xdr{} borrows the principle but changes the random
variable: entropy is over validator-positive execution modes, not tokens or a
known action set. Its replay and empirical advantages are an online surrogate,
not Soft Actor-Critic and not an exact gradient of global sequence entropy.

\paragraph{Executable outputs.}
Grammar constraints can prevent invalid generations \citep{geng2023grammar}.
We instead require execution to bind reward and identity. Python additionally
uses a restricted grammar before isolated execution; MathIR uses a finite
command language. This reduces false distinctions without claiming that every
open-ended reasoning strategy is executable.

\section{Experimental Design and Evidence Policy}
\label{sec:experiments}

\subsection{Matched training and evaluation}

All reported RL runs use Qwen2.5-0.5B-Instruct at a pinned revision, seeds
43--45, group size 16, learning rate $2\times10^{-7}$, one PPO epoch,
$\beta_{\mathrm{KL}}=0$, rollout temperature 1, top-$p=1$, maximum response
length 192, and at most 12 passes through a fixed training pool. Within a
domain, arms share the data, model, optimizer, prompt format, rollout count,
training budget, evaluation seed, checkpoint cadence, and recovery rules.

Evaluation uses greedy decoding and four deterministic replicates of $K=8$
temperature-one samples. Registered ModeBench checkpoints are passes
$0,1,2,3,4,5,6,8,10,12$. A point appears only when all three seeds in both
compared arms have landed it; faster seeds are never carried forward.

\subsection{Evidence hierarchy}

The registered ModeBench campaign pairs matched Dr.GRPO and \xdr{} across four
domains, with actuator-off/on comparisons planned across the same benchmark.
Infrastructure interruptions left the broad campaign incomplete. We therefore
do not report partial AUC, impute missing cells, or call the broad comparison
terminal.

\begin{table}[H]
  \centering
  \small
  \begin{tabularx}{\linewidth}{@{}P{.22\linewidth}YP{.11\linewidth}P{.22\linewidth}@{}}
    \toprule
    \textbf{Question} & \textbf{Comparison} & \textbf{Status} & \textbf{Allowed claim} \\
    \midrule
    Does \xdr{} oppose collapse across domains? &
    4 domains, 2 arms, 3 seeds; latest shared registered checkpoint &
    exploratory & direction and magnitude at the frozen surface \\
    Does isolated support discovery act causally? &
    MathIR, same-plumbing actuator off/on, 3 seeds, pass 12 &
    terminal & domain-specific mechanism result \\
    \bottomrule
  \end{tabularx}
  \caption{\textbf{Evidence hierarchy.} Scope is determined by completion and
  prospective controls, not by whether a result is favorable.}
  \label{tab:evidence}
\end{table}

\paragraph{Audit rules.}
Bank state, controller state, scheduler cursor, optimizer state, data cursor,
and request-stream state are checkpointed together. Non-finite values, state
mismatch, algorithm exceptions, missing terminal markers, proposal leakage
into PPO, or proposal overlap with on-policy counts are fail-closed violations.
Placement-only recovery amendments are hash-bound. Two superseded cohorts are
excluded by machine-readable objective audits rather than by their outcomes.

\section{Results}
\label{sec:results}

\subsection{Matched Dr.GRPO reaches a single-mode regime}

At the frozen shared checkpoints, matched Dr.GRPO has
\texttt{excess@8}$\leq .042$ in every domain (Table~\ref{tab:main-results}).
Thus, conditional on finding a correct answer, its eight samples almost never
contain an additional executed solution. The phenomenon is not simply low
accuracy. In Graph coloring, greedy accuracy is $.344$ both at initialization
and pass 10, while \texttt{excess@8} falls from $.190$ to $.021$. In Countdown,
accuracy rises substantially but excess breadth remains only $.042$. Python
converges to the same $.172$ on greedy, \texttt{mean@8}, \texttt{pass@8}, and
\texttt{distinct@8} for every seed. MathIR's
\texttt{distinct@8} and \texttt{pass@8} are both $.364$. These are the operational signatures
of concentration or failure to escape a singleton correct mode.

\subsection{\xdr{} improves both search success and executed breadth}

\begin{table}[H]
  \centering
  \small
  \setlength{\tabcolsep}{4.2pt}
  \begin{tabular}{@{}lrrrrr@{}}
    \toprule
    & & \multicolumn{2}{c}{\textbf{matched Dr.GRPO}} &
    \multicolumn{2}{c}{\textbf{\xdr{}}} \\
    \cmidrule(lr){3-4}\cmidrule(lr){5-6}
    \textbf{Domain} & \textbf{pass} & \texttt{pass@8} & \texttt{excess@8} &
    \texttt{pass@8} & \texttt{excess@8} \\
    \midrule
    Graph coloring & 10 & .372 & .021 & \textbf{.968} & \textbf{1.376} \\
    Countdown & 5 & .608 & .042 & \textbf{.665} & \textbf{1.010} \\
    Python factors & 6 & .172 & .000 & \textbf{.740} & \textbf{.216} \\
    MathIR action menu & 8 & .364 & .000 & \textbf{.779} & \textbf{.026} \\
    \bottomrule
  \end{tabular}
  \caption{\textbf{Exploratory shared-checkpoint means.} Excess is
  \texttt{distinct@8}$-$\texttt{pass@8}; it counts correct execution modes
  beyond the first. All entries use three seeds and four fixed sampling
  replicates.}
  \label{tab:main-results}
\end{table}

\xdr{} raises \texttt{pass@8} in every paired seed of every domain at the
reported surface. The largest breadth effects are in Graph
($+.595$ \texttt{pass@8}, $+1.355$ \texttt{excess@8}) and Countdown
($+.057$, $+.967$). Python improves strongly on average but is seed-unstable:
two treatment seeds reach $1.000$ \texttt{pass@8}, while the third reaches
$.219$; only one seed produces excess multiplicity at this checkpoint. MathIR
more than doubles \texttt{pass@8}, but most of the gain is finding a first
correct route rather than multiple routes. The result supports a consistent
direction across four validators, not a completed campaign-wide effect
estimate.

